 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>David Koma has kept the Blumarine girl in the grip of dark romanticism so far. Apparently she needed a holiday. So this time he sent her to California.</p><p>The journey began with a research trip to Los Angeles, a place Koma says he visits at least once a year to reset his brain. There, the Blumarine girl was imagined stepping out of the shadows and into the Californian sun: less gothic heroine, more ingenue, more beach-to-boudoir fantasy. Koma’s own description was straightforward: She goes to California, dates a surfer, and has a good time.</p><p>The approach was deliberately lightweight but not exactly a departure. By his own admission, Koma remains a control freak, and he built the collection around the friction between his instinct for precision and the looseness of the fantasy. Beachwear morphed into eveningwear; eveningwear was made to look cheekily undone. Somewhere between the two, innocence got a little lost. Tulle, transparency, and lingerie-minded silhouettes supplied the boudoir, while surf references supplied the beach. The point, apparently, was not to choose.</p><p>The California in question was less postcard perfect than culturally assembled: early-2000s Los Angeles with hints of the racy fantasy of ’70s <em>Playboy</em>. Koma was careful to distinguish it from nostalgic Y2K, though the distinction became rather academic once the surf T-shirts and retro sexiness started piling up.</p><p>Color provided another way out of the dark. Pastels appeared, but sun faded rather than sugary, with enough saturation to retain what Koma called a “kick.” Flounces, ruffles, and an abundance of sheer lace barely concealed a streak of kink. Koma is partial to the tension between release and control. He hasn’t abandoned the sharpness he considers his signature; he has simply loosened the corset a little.</p></div>",
